    experienceLevel: "5–8 Years",
    companies: ["Google","Meta","Amazon","Microsoft","Netflix","Adobe","Airbnb","Stripe","Atlassian","Uber"],
    category: "State Architecture",
    expectedAnswer: "**How does TanStack Query change state architecture?** changes the architecture by treating remote data as server state with caching, invalidation, retries, and synchronization rather than as ordinary client-owned state.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Clarify the requirement.\n\n**How does TanStack Query change state architecture?** changes the architecture by treating remote data as server state with caching, invalidation, retries, and synchronization rather than as ordinary client-owned state.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nStart with users, functional requirements, non-functional requirements, scale assumptions, state/data flow, and failure modes. Choose the smallest architecture that satisfies the constraints.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core decision, state transition, lookup, or contract so you can explain the reasoning line by line in a system-design interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface RecordItem {\n  id: string;\n  value: string;\n}\n\nfunction mergeRecord(\n  current: readonly RecordItem[],\n  incoming: RecordItem,\n): RecordItem[] {\n  const result: RecordItem[] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < current.length; i += 1) {\n    if (current[i].id !== incoming.id) {\n      result.push(current[i]);\n    }\n  }\n\n  result.push(incoming);\n  return result;\n}\n\nconst next = mergeRecord(\n  [{ id: \"1\", value: \"old\" }],\n  { id: \"1\", value: \"new\" },\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or platform helpers when they communicate the same architecture clearly and do not hide an important trade-off.\n\n```ts\nconst current = [\n  { id: \"1\", value: \"old\" },\n];\n\nconst incoming = {\n  id: \"1\",\n  value: \"new\",\n};\n\nconst next = [\n  ...current.filter(\n    (item) => item.id !== incoming.id,\n  ),\n  incoming,\n];\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nApply **How does TanStack Query change state architecture?** only to the state category it solves. Keep derived state derived and define cache/invalidation or update semantics explicitly.\n\nStep 6 — Complexity and scale:\nExplain the dominant read/write/render/network cost, the expected growth path, and where caching, partitioning, batching, pagination, or independent deployment would become useful.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases:\nHandle empty data, partial failure, retries, stale state, unauthorized access, slow networks, large datasets, migration constraints, and rollback where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the boundary, the source of truth, the failure/recovery path, the observability signal, and why this design is simpler or safer than the alternatives.",
